@baseUrl = http://localhost:9001

# All assets endpoints require authentication.
# Run "Login" from sessions/sessions.http first — the HTTP client stores and
# sends the cookie automatically on subsequent requests to the same host.

### List all assets  [protected]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/assets
Accept: application/json

### Create asset  [protected]
# @name createAsset
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/assets
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "title": "Building a Real-Time Risk Engine in Rust: Lessons from Production",
    "content": "[{\"id\":\"1\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\",\"level\":2},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"How We Built a Real-Time Risk Engine in Rust\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"2\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"When our legacy Java-based risk engine started buckling under peak-hour load — GC pauses spiking to 200ms during volatile market sessions — we knew it was time for a different approach. This is the story of rebuilding our real-time risk calculation pipeline in Rust, the tradeoffs we made, and what we learned after six months in production.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"3\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\",\"level\":3},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The Problem: Tail Latency Kills\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"4\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Average latency is a vanity metric in risk management. What matters is p99 and p999 — the worst-case scenarios that coincide with exactly the moments you need your risk engine most: flash crashes, earnings surprises, and sudden liquidity gaps. Our Java service delivered 2ms median latency but ballooned to 200ms at the tail. Traders were flying blind during the moments that mattered most.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"5\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\",\"level\":3},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Architecture Overview\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"6\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The engine ingests a stream of market data ticks and position updates via a lock-free SPSC ring buffer. Each tick triggers a recalculation of Greeks, VaR, and portfolio-level exposure across all active positions. Results are published to downstream dashboards and alerting systems over a zero-copy shared memory interface. The entire hot path allocates zero heap memory after initialization.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"7\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\",\"level\":3},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Crate Choices That Made a Difference\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"8\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"We leaned heavily on a handful of battle-tested crates. crossbeam for concurrent data structures and scoped threads. serde with rkyv for zero-copy deserialization of position snapshots. ndarray for vectorized numerical computation across the options pricing models. tokio was deliberately excluded from the hot path — async overhead is unacceptable at the microsecond scale — but powers the administrative API and monitoring endpoints.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"9\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\",\"level\":3},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The Hardest Part: Convincing Compliance\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"10\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Regulators and internal audit teams want to see deterministic, reproducible outputs from risk systems. Rust actually helped here. The absence of undefined behavior, combined with strict type safety and exhaustive pattern matching on enums representing market states, made it far easier to demonstrate to compliance that the engine would behave identically given identical inputs — something that was harder to guarantee in the Java codebase with its mutable shared state.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"11\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\",\"level\":3},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Results After Six Months\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"12\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The numbers speak for themselves. Median latency dropped from 2ms to 8 microseconds. P99 latency went from 200ms to 42 microseconds — a nearly 5000x improvement at the tail. Memory footprint shrank from 12GB to 800MB. CPU utilization under peak load fell by 60%, freeing capacity on the same hardware. Most importantly, we have had zero memory-safety-related production incidents since launch.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"13\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\",\"level\":3},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"What We Would Do Differently\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"14\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"We underestimated the learning curve for lifetime annotations in complex data structures that reference market data across multiple calculation cycles. If we started over, we would invest more upfront in designing a clean ownership hierarchy and use arena allocators from day one rather than retrofitting them later. We would also set up property-based testing with proptest earlier — it caught subtle numerical edge cases in our Greeks calculations that unit tests missed entirely.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"15\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Rust is not a silver bullet, but for latency-critical financial systems where correctness is non-negotiable, it is the best tool available today. The compiler is demanding, but in finance, a demanding compiler is far cheaper than a demanding regulator.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]}]",
    "tag_ids": []
}

> {% client.global.set("assetId", response.body.id) %}

### Create asset — missing title (expects 400)  [protected]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/assets
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": "[{\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"No title\",\"styles\":{}}]}]"
}

### Create asset — missing content (expects 400)  [protected]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/assets
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "No Content"
}

### Get asset by ID  [protected]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/assets/{{assetId}}
Accept: application/json

### Get asset — non-existent ID (expects 404)  [protected]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/assets/nonexistent
Accept: application/json

### Update asset  [protected]
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/assets/{{assetId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Updated Asset Title",
  "content": "[{\"id\":\"1\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"props\":{\"level\":1,\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Updated heading\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]},{\"id\":\"2\",\"type\":\"paragraph\",\"props\":{\"textColor\":\"default\",\"backgroundColor\":\"default\",\"textAlignment\":\"left\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Updated body text.\",\"styles\":{}}],\"children\":[]}]",
  "tag_ids": []
}

### Update asset — missing content (expects 400)  [protected]
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/assets/{{assetId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Title Only"
}

### Update asset — non-existent ID (expects 404)  [protected]
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/assets/nonexistent
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Ghost Asset",
  "content": "[]"
}

### Delete asset  [protected]
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/assets/{{assetId}}

### Delete asset — non-existent ID (expects 404)  [protected]
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/assets/nonexistent

### Upload Markdown — single file  [protected]
# @name uploadMarkdown
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/content-bank/assets/upload
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=boundary

--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="sample.md"
Content-Type: text/markdown

< ./sample.md
--boundary--

> {% client.global.set("mdAssetId", response.body.results[0].asset_id) %}

### Upload Markdown — multiple files (batch)  [protected]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/content-bank/assets/upload
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=boundary

--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="sample.md"
Content-Type: text/markdown

< ./sample.md
--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="sample2.md"
Content-Type: text/markdown

< ./sample2.md
--boundary--

### Upload Markdown — mixed valid + non-.md (per-file failure, 201)  [protected]
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/content-bank/assets/upload
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=boundary

--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="sample.md"
Content-Type: text/markdown

< ./sample.md
--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="files"; filename="notes.txt"
Content-Type: text/plain

just plain text, not markdown
--boundary--

